<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    A command-line front end for the GdsII library: read a file, say what is in it, dump its records,
    read them back, validate, list layers, export SVG, extrude a 3D model - and `gds mcp`, which serves the
    library's layout tools to an AI model over the Model Context Protocol.

    Cross-platform without doing anything special - it is a portable .NET console app with no native
    dependency and nothing platform-specific in it. `dotnet tool install` works the same on Windows,
    macOS and Linux, and `dotnet run` works anywhere the SDK does. The packages below are managed code
    as well, so that stays true.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <!--Matches the library, which now matches the app.-->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>

    <!--And runs on whatever comes after it. A tool built against one major refuses to start on a machine
        that has only a later one - which is exactly how this was found, as a net8.0 build on a .NET 10
        machine - so rolling forward is what keeps an installed copy working across the next release
        instead of failing on an upgrade nobody connected to this.-->
    <RollForward>Major</RollForward>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>GdsII.Cli</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>gds</AssemblyName>

    <!--Installable with `dotnet tool install`, which is what makes it one command on every platform.-->
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>gds</ToolCommandName>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>GdsII.Cli</PackageId>
    <Title>gds</Title>
    <Description>Command-line tool for GDSII, OASIS and DXF layout files: inspect, dump, validate, convert between any two, run booleans, check against a design rule deck, trace nets, measure, and export SVG or a 3D model (STL, OBJ, GLTF, GLB) with each layer at its own height from a layermap. `gds mcp` lets an AI model such as Claude open, draw on, edit, check and save the layouts in one folder, through the Model Context Protocol.</Description>
    <PackageTags>gdsii;gds;oasis;dxf;layout;eda;vlsi;semiconductor;cad;cli;dotnet-tool;mcp;ai</PackageTags>

    <!--
      Five licenses, because `dotnet tool install` ships a bundle rather than a reference: the tool's
      package carries GdsII.dll - which has Clipper2 compiled into it - alongside its package
      dependencies' own assemblies. Everything physically in the .nupkg is named here, which is not what
      an expression usually has to do and is what packing as a tool makes true.

      Apache-2.0 is the MCP SDK's. Its project is moving from MIT to Apache-2.0, and code nobody has
      relicensed yet stays MIT - which this expression already names, for SharpGLTF and for Microsoft's
      packages the SDK brings with it.

      The version, author and repository links come from Directory.Build.props.
    -->
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Unlicense AND BSL-1.0 AND SGI-B-2.0 AND MIT AND Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>

    <!--The same icon the library and the app carry, so all three are one thing on a search page.-->
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>

    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>

    <!--No InvariantGlobalization: a layer name out of a layermap can be any text, and the tool should
        not be the reason it comes back wrong.-->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\wwwroot\icon-192.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\icon.png" Link="Resources\icon.png" />
    <None Include="..\UNLICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\docs\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <!--
      The MCP SDK's own license file, as its repository has it at the commit 2.2.0 was built from. Apache-2.0
      asks for the license itself to travel with the code, and the SDK's package carries no copy of it - so
      this one does. Into a folder that names it, as the library does with Clipper2's.
    -->
    <None Include="licenses\modelcontextprotocol\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="licenses\modelcontextprotocol\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\GdsII\GdsII.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Both of these are for `gds model`, and both stop here: GdsII itself stays dependency-free, which is
    the property that makes it worth depending on.

    They earn their place by doing what a console has no three.js for. LibTessDotNet triangulates the
    caps, and a GDSII boundary is regularly concave - sky130 carries eight, twelve and fourteen-point
    outlines that a fan from one corner turns inside out. SharpGLTF writes the glTF, which is JSON
    alongside a binary buffer with accessors, byte strides and alignment rules.

    STL and OBJ are written by hand instead. Both are a triangle list with a header, a few dozen lines
    each, and a dependency for that would be more to keep current than to write. Argument parsing is
    hand-rolled for the same reason, which also avoids pinning anyone to a command-line library's
    opinions about how a flag should look.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="LibTessDotNet" Version="1.1.15" />
    <PackageReference Include="SharpGLTF.Toolkit" Version="1.0.6" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    For `gds mcp`: the official C# SDK for the Model Context Protocol, which is how Claude Desktop, Claude
    Code and the other MCP clients start a tool and talk to it. The protocol is JSON-RPC with an
    initialization handshake, capability negotiation, cancellation and a version that moves; the SDK keeps
    up with all of that, and a hand-rolled copy would be one more thing to keep current.

    The Core package rather than ModelContextProtocol, which adds hosting and dependency injection on top:
    a server on standard input and output needs neither, and Core brings two small Microsoft packages with
    it where the other brings a host. The layout tools themselves are the library's - GdsII/Agent - so this
    is transport, and nothing about a layout is decided here.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="ModelContextProtocol.Core" Version="2.2.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
